      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, can u explain how a capacitor behaves differently in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also whats the formula for capacitive reactance? i need to know this for my exam tomorrow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor acts as a short circuit because the capacitor stores the charge and holds it until the voltage returns to zero, effectively bypassing the voltage source. In contrast, an AC circuit behaves differently due to the changing voltage and current. Capacitive reactance increases with the frequency of the AC, causing it to oppose changes in the voltage and current more.\n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance is Xc = 1 / (2πfC), where Xc is the capacitive reactance, f is the frequency of the AC, and C is the capacitance value. This formula shows that capacitive reactance increases with increasing frequency and decreases with increasing capacitance.",
